 "lineas": [
  {
   "texto": "A soft little tree root can lift a slab of concrete that weighs more than a car.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "It starts underground, right under the sidewalk.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "At night, the concrete cools, and water from the soil condenses under it.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So the space under a slab is wet, dark, and full of air. Perfect for roots.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "A root grows toward it, and spreads flat along the bottom of the concrete.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Every year, it adds a new ring of wood and gets a little thicker.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Thicker means pushing up. Slowly, but with huge pressure.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Concrete is great at handling squeezing, but terrible at bending.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So the slab tilts at a joint, or cracks right down the middle.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Cutting the root doesn't really work. It can kill the tree, and a new one comes back.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Cities now bury plastic root barriers that steer roots down, away from the slab.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Some use loose, airy soil deeper down, so roots have a better place to go.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "And some sidewalks are made of rubber, so they just bend over the root.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Slow and soft still wins, if you give it enough years. What should we explain next?",
   "pausa": 0.2
  }
 ],
